@startuml
' The two ways an editor's input reaches the database.
'
' They are different pipelines: a resource page owns the record's lifecycle, so
' the package hooks into the save. A modal action does not own it, so the host
' lifts the seo array out before the record is written and applies it after.

title Two ways to author metadata

skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #ffffff
  BorderColor #475569
  DiamondBackgroundColor #f8fafc
}

start

:Editor submits the form;

if (Is this a resource page?) then (yes)

    partition "Create / Edit page" {
        :Hydrate the seo fields on fill;
        :Strip the seo array from the data;
        :Save the record;
        :saveSeo() writes the related row;
    }

else (no)

    partition "Modal action or relation manager" {
        :captureSeoFormData() via mutateDataUsing();
        :Strip the seo array from the data;
        :The action creates or updates the record;
        :applyPendingSeoFormData() via after();
        :saveSeo() writes the related row;
    }

endif

stop

note left
  Both routes end in the same place:
  __saveSeo()__, which creates the row on
  first save and updates it in place after
  that. The seo array never reaches the
  model's own attributes.
end note

@enduml
